cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset, please cite it as below."
authors:
  - family-names: Williams
    given-names: Thomas C.
title: "Distributional Misery Index: Measuring Economic Pressure Across Income Groups"
version: 0.1.12
# date-released is intentionally left as the repair-branch placeholder below.
# It must be replaced with the actual v0.1.12 tag date when (and only when) the
# repair PR is merged to main and the release is cut. Until then, no v0.1.12
# tag exists.
date-released: 2026-08-15
url: "https://github.com/dmianalysis/dmi"
repository-code: "https://github.com/dmianalysis/dmi"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - economics
  - inflation
  - unemployment
  - income distribution
  - consumer expenditure
  - economic indicators
  - distributional analysis
  - misery index
abstract: >
  The Distributional Misery Index (DMI) measures monthly economic pressure
  across five income quintiles by combining group-specific inflation (weighted
  by U.S. Consumer Expenditure Survey patterns) with labor-market slack. The
  v0.1.12 release publishes two specifications: a Baseline series (U-3
  unemployment, headline CPI) and a Slack-Plus companion series (U-6
  unemployment, headline CPI). The previously advertised Core CPI specification
  was withdrawn in v0.1.12 because its inputs were derived from headline CPI
  and did not implement a bona fide core-inflation calculation
  (see docs/repair/CORE_WITHDRAWAL.md). v0.1.12 is a pre-1.0 exceptional
  breaking public-schema release.
references:
  - type: report
    title: "Consumer Expenditure Survey"
    authors:
      - name: "U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics"
    year: 2023
